# Line-ending policy for files whose *bytes* are part of a published claim.
#
# ── Why this file exists ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repository is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so by default Git checks text
# files out with CRLF. That is harmless for source, and it is not harmless for a generated data asset
# whose SHA-256 is recorded as an audit fact.
#
# `assets/data/city-catalogue.json` is committed alongside a checksum in
# `docs/THIRD_PARTY_LICENCES.md` and a reproducibility claim in `scripts/import-city-catalogue.mjs`:
# re-running the importer over the recorded GeoNames inputs must reproduce the committed file byte
# for byte. Under the default policy it would not. The asset holds 49 literal newlines, so a checkout
# on a Windows machine would rewrite it to 2,297,052 bytes with an entirely different hash — while a
# checkout on Linux produced the documented 2,297,003. The audit would then pass or fail depending on
# who ran it, which is the opposite of what a checksum is for.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree form to the form the importer emits, on every platform. The file
# stays diffable text; only its newline convention is fixed.
assets/data/*.json text eol=lf

# ── Source and configuration: LF in the repository *and* in the working tree ──
#
# Added 2026-08-18. The paragraph above says a CRLF checkout "is harmless for
# source". That was true when it was written and is no longer true, because
# `.prettierrc.json` pins `endOfLine: "lf"`. With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows
# checkout rewrites LF blobs to CRLF in the working tree, and `format:check` then
# fails on 41 files whose committed bytes are already correct — a gate that cannot
# pass on one platform and passes on another, for reasons unrelated to the code.
#
# The rules below change no stored blob. Every one of these files is already LF in
# the repository; `eol=lf` only pins the form Git writes into the working tree, so
# a checkout stops introducing the difference. Nothing is renormalised.
#
# Extensions are enumerated rather than using `* text=auto`, so no binary asset
# (png, ttf, mp3, jpg, ico, webp) can be reclassified as text by accident, and
# `.ps1` is deliberately absent: PowerShell scripts keep the platform default.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
